A.6.1.1.2.2.6.1.2.2.1.3.2 defines two overloads. The full one takes `lastAmount` and `lastUpdated` explicitly, guarded by `require(lastAmount <= maxAmount)` and `require(lastUpdated <= block.timestamp)`. The convenience one is:

```
function setRateLimitData(bytes32 key, uint256 maxAmount, uint256 slope) external override {
    setRateLimitData(key, maxAmount, slope, maxAmount, block.timestamp);
}
```

It starts the bucket **full**. So an administrative adjustment is not only a change to future capacity — it silently restores present capacity, discarding any consumption that had occurred since the last update. A key that had been drained to zero by legitimate activity becomes fully available the moment its parameters are touched, even if the change was a reduction in `maxAmount`.

This is a governance hazard rather than a code defect: a routine "lower this limit" action can transiently *increase* available capacity relative to a moment earlier. Anyone tightening a limit in response to an ongoing incident needs the four-argument overload with an explicit `lastAmount`, not the convenient one.

The backdating allowance in the full overload is the other side of the same design. Permitting `lastUpdated` in the past lets an admin set a limit that presents as already partially replenished — useful for migrations that must not create a discontinuity, and a capability that requires the same care.
